No user-reported issues just yet, but it is for example failing to deserialize the following example correctly:

```C#
Base result = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<Base>("""{"naïve" : "derived" }""");
Console.WriteLine(result is Derived); // False

[JsonPolymorphic(TypeDiscriminatorPropertyName = "naïve")]
[JsonDerivedType(typeof(Derived), "derived")]
public record Base;
public record Derived : Base;
```

I can revert this if you feel that it's out of scope, but this seems to be a bug in my view.

> If the necessary type discriminator is first, today it's valid, there's no buffering, and tomorrow there wouldn't be buffering either. 

It would buffer even in that scenario since it would want to look ahead for other metadata properties such as `$id` or `$values`.

> Where am I getting that wrong?

Consider the hierarchy:

```C#
[JsonDerivedType(typeof(Derived), "derived"))]
public record Base(IEnumerable<int> values);
public record Derived(int x) : Base([x]);
```

This makes type discriminators legal because you have Derived but also optional since `Base` doesn't specify one. In that case you could today write something like
```C#
var value = new Base(Enumerable.Repeat(1, int.MaxValue));
using fs = File.OpenWrite("/some/path");
JsonSerializer.Serialize(fs, value); // { "values" : [1, 1, ... ] }
```
Currently the above can serialize and deserialize without any overbuffering whatsoever, however assuming we turned on reading ahead by default then it would try to buffer the entire payload so that it can look for the optional `$type` declaration.
